        {
          "id": "cgs2020e1-q8",
          "type": "mc",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "stem": "The area enclosed by the curves \\(y=\\sqrt{4-x^2}\\) and \\(y=x^2-2\\) is shaded in the diagram below. Which expression could be used to calculate this area?",
          "options": [
            "\\(2\\int_0^1\\left(\\sqrt{4-x^2}-x^2+2\\right)\\,dx\\)",
            "\\(\\int_2^{-2}\\left(\\sqrt{4-x^2}-x^2-2\\right)\\,dx\\)",
            "\\(2\\int_0^{\\sqrt3}\\left(\\sqrt{4-x^2}-x^2+2\\right)\\,dx\\)",
            "\\(\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{\\sqrt3}\\left(\\sqrt{4-x^2}-x^2-2\\right)\\,dx\\)"
          ],
          "answer": 2,
          "hint": "Find the curves’ intersection points, identify which curve is above the other, and use symmetry about the y-axis.",
          "keyPoints": [
            {
              "text": "\\(y=\\sqrt{4-x^2}\\)",
              "note": "This is the upper semicircle and is the upper curve between the intersections."
            },
            {
              "text": "\\(y=x^2-2\\)",
              "note": "Subtract this lower curve from the semicircle."
            },
            {
              "text": "shaded",
              "note": "The enclosed region is symmetric about the y-axis."
            }
          ],
          "steps": [
            {
              "explain": "At an intersection, set the two y-values equal. Because the square-root side is nonnegative, retain only solutions with \\(x^2\\ge2\\).",
              "work": "\\(\\sqrt{4-x^2}=x^2-2\\Rightarrow4-x^2=(x^2-2)^2\\Rightarrow x^4-3x^2=0\\Rightarrow x=\\pm\\sqrt3\\)",
